WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.400
How does Peterson’s algorithm resolve

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.520
a tie? It combines two

3
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.060
interest flags with a turn

4
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.600
variable, so each process announces

5
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:09.940
its wish to enter while

6
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.180
allowing the other to win

7
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.860
simultaneous contention. We use the

8
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.880
course two-process model with atomic

9
00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.140
reads and writes and the

10
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.400
stated ordering assumptions.

11
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.380
For process i, set its

12
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.300
own flag true, set turn

13
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.060
to the other process k,

14
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.020
then wait while the other

15
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:28.480
flag is true and turn

16
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.680
favors that other process. On

17
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.760
exit, clear the process own

18
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.120
flag. The conjunction in the

19
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.220
waiting condition is essential.

20
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.780
Start a simultaneous attempt by

21
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.320
letting P zero set flag

22
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.100
zero true and turn one.

23
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.300
P one then sets flag

24
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.140
one true and turn zero.

25
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.380
Both flags now express interest,

26
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:51.860
but the last turn value

27
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.160
resolves who must defer.

28
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.760
P zero checks flag one

29
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.740
and whether turn equals one.

30
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.720
Flag one is true but

31
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.680
turn is zero, so the

32
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.120
conjunction is false and P

33
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.600
zero enters. P one sees

34
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.280
flag zero true and turn

35
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.240
zero, so its guard remains

36
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.500
true and it waits.

37
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.760
When P zero leaves, it

38
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.280
clears flag zero. P one

39
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.800
now sees the other flag

40
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.520
false, so it may enter

41
00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.220
regardless of the retained turn

42
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.780
value. The exit action releases

43
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.080
the waiting competitor without requiring

44
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.520
both flags to be reset

45
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.000
together.

46
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.740
If only P zero wants

47
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.380
entry, P one keeps its

48
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.980
flag false. Even though P

49
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.360
zero gives turn to P

50
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.400
one, the false interest flag

51
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.200
makes the waiting conjunction false.

52
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.060
This avoids the strict-alternation failure

53
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.280
where an uninterested process could

54
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.220
hold the turn forever.

55
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.400
For exclusion, suppose a process

56
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.260
is already inside with its

57
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.880
flag true. A competitor announces

58
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.640
itself and gives turn to

59
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.740
the current process, making the

60
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.300
competitor guard wait. Under the

61
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.380
course ordering assumptions, the flag

62
00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.400
and turn argument prevents both

63
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.880
from passing into their critical

64
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.780
sections together.

65
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.780
Progress follows because an absent

66
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.520
competitor has a false flag,

67
00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.420
while simultaneous interested processes use

68
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.640
turn to select one. Bounded

69
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.600
overtaking follows from that reciprocal

70
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.300
turn behavior under the assumed

71
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.460
execution model. It is a

72
00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.720
logical protocol proof, not a

73
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.940
promise that arbitrary compiled code

74
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:24.840
obeys the model.

75
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.160
Do not reorder the announcement

76
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.760
and guard merely because the

77
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.300
lines look interchangeable. The photographed

78
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.860
exercises deliberately change ordering or

79
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.420
scheduling to test the reasoning.

80
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.360
On modern processors, compiler and

81
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.620
memory reordering also require proper

82
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.900
language-level synchronization; ordinary variables are

83
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.000
not automatically a portable production

84
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:46.260
lock.

85
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.020
Things to take note of.

86
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:49.820
Write the waiting condition with

87
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.300
both the other flag and

88
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.480
the turn comparison, clear your

89
00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.280
own flag on exit, and

90
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.720
trace simultaneous interest explicitly. Separate

91
00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.120
the course proof from claims

92
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.520
about an unqualified modern implementation.
